<figure class="thread-media thread-media--linked"><a href="/assets/archive/communication/folhateen/capafolhateen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open the Folhateen cover at full size"><img src="/assets/media/thread/folhateen-cover-2001.webp" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Folhateen cover with the headline Órfãos do Rock, April 23, 2001 issue."></a><figcaption><strong>Folhateen — “Órfãos do Rock”, Apr. 23, 2001.</strong><span class="thread-media-note">Cover digitally restored with AI assistance from the preserved original copy.</span><a class="thread-media-open" href="/assets/archive/communication/folhateen/capafolhateen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open full-size image ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
<p>On April 23, 2001, Marcelo Nicchio appeared in the <strong>cover story of Folhateen</strong>, the youth supplement of <em>Folha de S.Paulo</em>, published after Joey Ramone's death. The printed cover line was <strong>“Órfãos do Rock”</strong> (“Orphans of Rock”); the inside article preserved in Folha's official digital archive was titled <strong>“Onde estão os ídolos?”</strong> (“Where are the idols?”).</p>
<p>The article records his comments on Joey Ramone, punk culture, the do-it-yourself principle, the Brazilian underground market and the persistence of rock.</p>
